# ===========================================================================
# WHAT MUST NOT BE REWRITTEN - AND DELIBERATELY NOTHING ELSE
# ===========================================================================
#
# Reference: `Workshop/tsubasa-git/.gitattributes`, which opens with
# `* text=auto eol=lf` because that project round-trips subtitle BYTES and 66%
# of the real corpus is CRLF.
#
# 🚨 THAT LINE WAS WRITTEN HERE FIRST AND THEN MEASURED, AND THE MEASUREMENT
# TOOK IT OUT AGAIN. Twelve files in this tree are CRLF today -- eight source
# modules including hato/pipeline.py (1,499 lines), hato/client.py,
# hato/doctor.py, hato/episodes.py and hato/report.py, plus three test files
# and LEDGER.md. `text=auto` normalises line endings in the INDEX, so the next
# `git add` would have produced a whole-file diff on every one of them, while
# two other builders were editing this tree and the workspace auto-commits
# every few minutes. A cosmetic rule would have collided with live work.
#
# Normalising this tree's line endings is a legitimate one-shot job. It is not
# a thing to start during a parallel build, and it is not this file's job.
# So: there is NO blanket rule. Every line below protects a specific file
# whose BYTES OR ENDINGS ARE PART OF WHAT IT IS.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE WINDOWS LAUNCHER MUST BE CRLF ON EVERY CHECKOUT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `hato-run.cmd` is 8,990 bytes with 187 CRLF endings - measured, not assumed.
# It is the only user-facing artifact that is not a Python entry point:
# double-clicked, dragged onto, and what Task Scheduler runs
# (spec/10-deployment.md). cmd.exe is only MOSTLY tolerant of LF endings, and
# the place that would surface is the scheduled run - the one nobody watches.
#
# `text eol=crlf` rather than `-text` on purpose: it guarantees CRLF on every
# checkout, including one made on Linux by an editor that saves LF. It costs a
# one-time index rewrite of this single file, which nothing is editing (RUNBOOK
# step 5c is done). One file is a cost worth paying; twelve was not.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. THE RECORDED API FIXTURES ARE BYTE-EXACT, AND A HASH SAYS SO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every capture under `tests/fixtures/` is the response body BYTE FOR BYTE,
# beside a `<stem>.meta.json` recording the date, URL, status, response
# headers and the body's sha256 - and `test_doctor.py` fails when a body no
# longer matches its recorded hash (spec/07-test-plan.md).
#
# Measured today: every body is single-line JSON, so a conversion would be a
# no-op RIGHT NOW. That is luck, not protection - `hato doctor --capture`
# writes whatever jimaku sends, and the first multi-line body committed
# unprotected would fail its own hash check on another platform, for a reason
# no assertion names.
#
# `-text` rather than `binary`: no conversion either way, but the diff stays
# readable, because re-capturing a fixture is a reviewable event.
tests/fixtures/**   -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. NO SUBTITLE FILE CAN BE COMMITTED HERE - THIS IS THE SECOND NET
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.gitignore` blocks these outright: subtitle content carries no stated
# licence and is not ours to redistribute, and every subtitle the suites touch
# is built in a temp dir. These lines exist only so a deliberate force-add
# cannot ALSO silently rewrite the bytes of the thing being added.
*.srt       -text
*.ass       -text
*.ssa       -text
*.vtt       -text
*.sub       -text
*.idx       -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LICENCE IS A FETCHED CANONICAL FILE, NOT OURS TO REFLOW
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 35,149 bytes, sha256
# 3972dc9744f6499f0f9b2dbf76696f2ae7ad8af9b23dde66d6af86c9dfb36986, taken
# straight from GitHub's licences API and byte-identical to the engine's copy.
# Marked so that hash stays checkable by anyone who clones this.
LICENSE     -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub language statistics - this is a Python project, not a docs project
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
spec/*.md   linguist-documentation
